# ============================================================================
# BuildFlow server configuration
#
#   cp .env.example .env      then edit .env (this file is the committed template)
#
# Loaded automatically at boot by src/loadEnv.ts (Node's built-in env loader).
# .env holds secrets — it is gitignored. Never commit it.
#
# The server boots in LOG MODE (emails only print to the console) until an SMTP
# provider below is enabled. The startup banner tells you which mode you're in
# and verifies the connection, so a bad setup fails loudly instead of silently.
# ============================================================================

# ---- Always-on settings (apply to every provider) --------------------------
# From: header on every email. Use an address on a domain you've verified with
# your provider, or sends may be rejected.
SMTP_FROM=BuildFlow <hello@yourdomain.com>
# Inbox that receives new sales leads from the Contact Sales form.
SALES_EMAIL=sales@yourdomain.com
# Inbox for the in-app "Give feedback" tab, attachments included. Unset, this falls back to
# the address hard-coded in app.ts, which is a personal one — set it before launch.
# Whoever it is, the route is capped at 10 an hour per customer (per address for visitors on the
# shared demo), because it is the one outward-sending route a read-only demo can still use and it
# carries up to three 10MB attachments.
# FEEDBACK_EMAIL=feedback@yourdomain.com

# URLs used inside emails (public site link in waitlist mail).
# BUILDFLOW_CLIENT_URL is a SECURITY setting on a public address, not just a cosmetic one: it is the
# origin put into emailed verification, invite and password-reset links. Left unset, that origin comes
# from the caller's own Origin header — which any client can set to anything, so a stranger can make
# BuildFlow email one of your users a real reset link pointing at their site. Set, it wins over the
# header, and a header is then only accepted when it matches. Unset in production, the server warns.
BUILDFLOW_PUBLIC_URL=https://buildflow.com/
BUILDFLOW_CLIENT_URL=http://localhost:5175/
# Where this API answers, for links that must point back at it. Defaults to the request's own
# host, which is wrong behind a proxy that rewrites it.
# BUILDFLOW_API_URL=https://api.buildflow.com

# Guards POST /api/waitlist/announce. Leave unset to keep the broadcast disabled.
WAITLIST_ADMIN_TOKEN=

PORT=4300

# ============================================================================
# DATA LAYER — migrations & backups
#
#   Schema changes are versioned migrations (SQLite user_version) applied to the
#   main DB and every per-tenant data/org-<id>.sqlite on open — nothing to run by
#   hand. Backups snapshot the DBs into data/backups/ (pruned to BACKUP_RETAIN).
#   A snapshot is taken at boot; set BACKUP_INTERVAL_MIN>0 for periodic snapshots,
#   or trigger on demand: POST /api/ops/backup.
#
#   WHERE the data lives is BUILDFLOW_DATA_FILE. Everything follows it: the
#   per-workspace databases sit beside it, backups go in backups/ next to it, and
#   the restore CLI reads the same value, so the server and the CLI cannot end up
#   disagreeing about where the data is. A relative path resolves against the
#   working directory; unset means server/data/buildflow.sqlite, resolved from the
#   server's own files so it does not depend on where the process was started.
#
#   This matters on a host whose filesystem is not durable. docs/replit.md records
#   that a published Replit app does not keep the files it writes, so the accounts
#   made on it are lost on the next publish. Pointing this at a path that outlives
#   a deploy is the smallest thing that helps. The real fix is DATABASE_URL: with
#   it set, every data file is also kept in PostgreSQL and loaded back at startup
#   (server/src/fileDurability.ts; Replit provides it with a production database).
#
#   BUILDFLOW_REQUIRE_DATABASE=1 makes a missing DATABASE_URL stop the start
#   instead of falling back to local files. .replit sets it on the published
#   app's start command only; leave it unset on a laptop and in the workspace.
# ============================================================================
# BUILDFLOW_DATA_FILE=/var/lib/buildflow/buildflow.sqlite
# BUILDFLOW_REQUIRE_DATABASE=1

# ============================================================================
# THE PAGE'S SECURITY HEADERS
#
#   When this server also serves the built pages (serveClient.ts, production),
#   the page carries a Content-Security-Policy and a Permissions-Policy. The
#   policy names the hosts the site really loads from: Google Fonts, the two
#   image hosts the marketing pages hotlink photographs from, and the three
#   analytics hosts, which are listed although analytics ships off so that
#   turning it on does not break instead of working.
#
#   Turning analytics on for a provider not listed there, or adding a font or
#   image host, needs the policy widened in serveClient.ts. CONTENT_SECURITY_POLICY=off
#   removes the header, which is the escape hatch if something in production is
#   blocked and waiting for a code change is not an option. Prefer widening it.
#
#   HSTS is sent only when the request arrived over https, which behind a proxy
#   needs TRUST_PROXY set as well.
# ============================================================================
# CONTENT_SECURITY_POLICY=off
# BACKUP_RETAIN=20
# BACKUP_INTERVAL_MIN=0
# Guards /api/ops/* (backup + list). Unset → localhost only, and OFF entirely when
# NODE_ENV=production: a deployed API usually sits behind a proxy on its own host, so
# every request looks local and that fallback would open these routes to everyone.
# Set this in production. It also guards GET /api/ops/stats — runtime counters: requests by
# route pattern and status class, a duration histogram, whole-file database rewrites and the
# bytes they cost, and process memory. JSON by default, Prometheus text with
# ?format=prometheus. It holds counts only: no org, no account, no path parameters.
# OPS_ADMIN_TOKEN=

# ============================================================================
# DEPLOYMENT — proxies and browser origins
# ============================================================================
# Set ONLY if a load balancer / reverse proxy sits in front of this API. It tells
# Express how many hops to trust when reading the client address out of
# X-Forwarded-For; every rate limit (signup, login, "email me a reset link") is
# keyed on that address. Unset is the safe default — the socket address is used and
# a client cannot pick its own bucket. Use the NUMBER of proxies you actually run
# ("1" is the common case); "true" behind a proxy that does not overwrite the header
# lets a client spoof the address again.
# TRUST_PROXY=1
#
# Where the auth rate limits are counted. UNSET = in this process, which is correct for a
# single API and wrong for two: each instance keeps its own counters, so N instances behind
# a load balancer means N times the allowance and "five wrong passwords locks the email"
# becomes five PER INSTANCE. Set this once you run more than one.
# An unreachable Redis is not an outage — the limits fall back to per-process counting and
# the server says so once in the log.
# REDIS_URL=redis://127.0.0.1:6379
# REDIS_URL=rediss://user:password@cache.example.com:6380/0   # TLS, auth, database 0
#
# Comma-separated browser origins allowed to make credentialed calls. Any localhost
# port is allowed automatically OFF production; in production this list is the only
# thing accepted, so set it to the client's real origin.
# CORS_ORIGIN=https://app.yourcompany.com
#
# Request logging: off | text | json. Default is text, except under test. One line per
# request with method, path, status, duration, a request id (also returned as the
# X-Request-Id header) and the workspace once the request has one. A healthy /api/health
# is not logged; a failing one is. The QUERY STRING IS NEVER WRITTEN — the calendar feed
# key, OAuth codes and one-time tokens all travel there — only parameter names are kept.
# Use json behind a log aggregator.
# REQUEST_LOG=text

# ============================================================================
# NOTIFICATIONS — delays / new assignments / crew conflicts
#
#   Alerts the PMs on an org's account (login emails) when a delay is reported, a
#   crew is assigned, or an assignment double-books a crew. Channels come from
#   NOTIFY_CHANNELS (default: email, which uses the email transport above). SMS
#   (Twilio) and push LOG until configured. Add OPS_NOTIFY_EMAIL/SMS to also reach
#   a shared ops inbox / on-call number (users & crews have no contact fields yet).
# ============================================================================
# NOTIFY_CHANNELS=email          # comma list: email,sms,push
# OPS_NOTIFY_EMAIL=ops@yourcompany.com
# OPS_NOTIFY_SMS=+15555550123    # comma-separated E.164 numbers
#
# ---- SMS via Twilio (fill all three to send; else SMS logs) ----
# TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=AC...
# TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=your-auth-token
# TWILIO_FROM=+15555550100        # or TWILIO_MESSAGING_SERVICE_SID=MG...
#
# ---- Web push (VAPID) — logs until keys + client subscriptions are wired ----
# VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
# VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=

# ============================================================================
# BUILDFLOW AI  (Claude)  —  optional; "Ask BuildFlow AI" uses built-in
# simulated answers until this is set (DEMO MODE), then answers with real Claude.
#
#   Get a key at https://platform.claude.com  →  paste it below, restart the
#   server. The boot banner flips from "DEMO MODE" to "LIVE". No key = the app
#   still works, just with the canned demo answers.
# ============================================================================
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
# An OAuth token works in place of the API key; either one turns BuildFlow AI live.
# ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN=
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-opus-5       # optional override (default: claude-opus-5)
#   Opus 5 and Opus 4.8 cost the same ($5/$25 per MTok); claude-sonnet-5 is cheaper
#   ($2/$10) if the answers are good enough for your workspaces. Whatever you pin here,
#   ai.ts sets adaptive thinking explicitly — do not remove it, because on Opus 4.8 and
#   4.7 omitting it silently runs with thinking OFF.

# ============================================================================
# BILLING / PAYMENTS  (Stripe)  —  optional; plans are display-only until set
#
#   With no STRIPE_SECRET_KEY the pricing plans stay display-only and every
#   /api/billing/* endpoint responds "not configured" (no charges possible).
#   To accept subscriptions: create your products + recurring per-seat Prices in
#   the Stripe dashboard, then fill in the values below. Card data is handled
#   entirely by Stripe's hosted Checkout — it never touches this server.
#
#   Test mode uses sk_test_… keys; production uses sk_live_…  (keys:
#   https://dashboard.stripe.com/apikeys)
# ============================================================================
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_your_secret_key
#
# One recurring, per-seat (per_unit) Price per plan × billing period — create at
# https://dashboard.stripe.com/products. Yearly should reflect the 20% discount.
# STRIPE_PRICE_PRO_MONTHLY=price_...
# STRIPE_PRICE_PRO_YEARLY=price_...
# STRIPE_PRICE_BUSINESS_MONTHLY=price_...
# STRIPE_PRICE_BUSINESS_YEARLY=price_...
#
# Webhook signing secret so subscription status persists. Add an endpoint at
# <your-api>/api/billing/webhook in Stripe, or locally run:
#   stripe listen --forward-to localhost:4300/api/billing/webhook
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...

# ============================================================================
# NO-CREDENTIAL TEST DELIVERY  (Ethereal)
#
#   Want to SEE the emails actually send, without signing up for a provider?
#       EMAIL_MODE=ethereal
#   At boot the server provisions a throwaway nodemailer test inbox and does a
#   real SMTP round-trip for every send — no real recipients, no credentials.
#   Each send prints a "Preview:" URL to the server console; open it to view the
#   fully rendered email. Requires outbound network. Ignored the moment a real
#   SMTP_HOST is set below (real delivery always wins).
# ============================================================================
# EMAIL_MODE=ethereal
# The same thing by another name, kept because older setups use it: SMTP_ETHEREAL=1.
# SMTP_ETHEREAL=1

# ============================================================================
# SMTP PROVIDER  —  pick your delivery service
#
#   To send real email: UNCOMMENT EXACTLY ONE block below, then paste your
#   secret into that block's SMTP_PASS. Restart the server; the banner should
#   print "Email: LIVE … verified". Leave all blocks commented for LOG MODE.
#
#   Only one block may be active — if two are uncommented the LAST one wins.
# ============================================================================

# ----- Gmail / Google Workspace ---------------------------------------------
# SMTP_PASS is a 16-char App Password (Google Account → Security → App passwords;
# requires 2-Step Verification). It is NOT your normal login password.
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_SECURE=false
# SMTP_USER=you@yourdomain.com
# SMTP_PASS=your-16-char-app-password

# ----- SendGrid -------------------------------------------------------------
# SMTP_USER is the literal word "apikey". SMTP_PASS is your API key (starts SG.).
# Verify a sender or domain in SendGrid first, or mail bounces.
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.sendgrid.net
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_SECURE=false
# SMTP_USER=apikey
# SMTP_PASS=SG.your-api-key

# ----- Resend ---------------------------------------------------------------
# SMTP_USER is the literal word "resend". SMTP_PASS is your API key (starts re_).
# Verify your sending domain in the Resend dashboard first.
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.resend.com
# SMTP_PORT=465
# SMTP_SECURE=true
# SMTP_USER=resend
# SMTP_PASS=re_your-api-key

# ----- Postmark -------------------------------------------------------------
# SMTP_USER and SMTP_PASS are BOTH your Server API Token. The From: address
# (SMTP_FROM above) must be a verified Sender Signature.
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.postmarkapp.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_SECURE=false
# SMTP_USER=your-postmark-server-token
# SMTP_PASS=your-postmark-server-token

# ----- Mailgun --------------------------------------------------------------
# Credentials come from Mailgun → Sending → Domain settings → SMTP credentials.
# EU region: use smtp.eu.mailgun.org instead.
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.mailgun.org
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_SECURE=false
# SMTP_USER=postmaster@mg.yourdomain.com
# SMTP_PASS=your-mailgun-smtp-password

# ============================================================================
# WEEKLY DIGEST  —  Monday's "what changed this week" email, one per org
#
#   On by default. The startup banner says which day and hour it will run, and
#   GET /api/schedule/digest previews the content without sending anything.
# ============================================================================
# Anything other than "off" leaves it on.
# WEEKLY_DIGEST=off
# Day of the week to send on, 0=Sunday .. 6=Saturday. Default 1 (Monday).
# DIGEST_WEEKDAY=1
# Hour to send from, 0-23, server local time. Default 7.
# DIGEST_HOUR=7

# ============================================================================
# GOOGLE AND MICROSOFT  —  the Dashboard's Meetings calendar (and optional sign-in)
#
#   The Meetings panel's Google and Outlook buttons stay switched off until these
#   are set. Google and Microsoft only issue a client secret to the account that
#   owns the app, so register one app with each, once, in your own consoles
#   (the steps are at the top of src/calendar.ts), then restart the API.
#
#   Redirect URIs to register, on this API's own origin (http://localhost:4300
#   in development; BUILDFLOW_API_URL in production):
#     <API origin>/api/calendar/google/callback        Meetings, Google
#     <API origin>/api/calendar/microsoft/callback     Meetings, Outlook
#     <API origin>/api/auth/oauth/google/callback      sign-in with Google (optional)
#     <API origin>/api/auth/oauth/microsoft/callback   sign-in with Microsoft (optional)
#   Scopes asked for: Google calendar.readonly; Microsoft Calendars.Read and
#   offline_access. Read-only: BuildFlow never writes to anyone's calendar.
# ============================================================================
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID=
# MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET=
# "common" takes work, school and personal Microsoft accounts; a tenant id limits it to one organisation.
# MICROSOFT_TENANT=common
#
# Key that signs the short-lived cookie carrying the OAuth/Connect round trip. Unset, the server
# invents one per process, and the cookie then only verifies on the process that issued it: a
# restart or redeploy between the click and the provider's redirect refuses the sign-in, and a
# deployment running more than one instance refuses every one. Any long random string; keep it the
# same across restarts. The server warns at startup if a provider is set and this is not.
# BUILDFLOW_SECRET=

# ============================================================================
# TEST AND DEVELOPMENT ONLY  —  do not set either of these in production
# ============================================================================
# Under NODE_ENV=test the mailer never sends. Set this to let a test actually
# deliver, which is only useful when checking a provider's credentials by hand.
# BUILDFLOW_TEST_REAL_MAIL=1
#
# Returns auth tokens in API responses so a test can read them without an inbox.
# It is already on under NODE_ENV=test. Setting it anywhere else hands every
# caller the tokens that log people in — it exists for local debugging and
# nothing else.
# BUILDFLOW_EXPOSE_AUTH_TOKENS=1
